n \'cart\', maybe the next step is to check other services with high call counts. Alternatively, using get_operations on a specific service to see what operations are being performed. \n\nWait, the user\'s last tool call was get_dependency_graph, which gave the call counts. The response shows that \'load-generator\' calls \'frontend-proxy\' 106 times, which is a lot. Maybe that\'s a point of failure. But to confirm, they need to check the traces for \'frontend-proxy\' or \'load-generator\'. Alternatively, check the metrics for those services. \n\nBut the previous get_metrics attempts had issues. Maybe using get_traces on \'frontend-proxy\' or \'load-generator\' would help. Alternatively, check the deployments and pods to see if there are any issues with the number of replicas or if pods are crashing. Using exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get deployments or pods in the \'astronomy-shop\' namespace could be useful. \n\nWait, the user hasn\'t checked the deployments yet. Maybe some services have deployments with errors. For example, if a deployment has zero replicas or is in a failed state. So using kubectl to get deployments and check their status. Also, checking the pods to see if any are in CrashLoopBackOff or similar states. \n\nSo the next step could be to use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get deployments in the namespace. That would help identify if any services have deployment issues. Then, check the pods for each deployment to see their status. If a pod is failing, that could be the root cause. \n\nAlternatively, if the dependency graph shows high call counts, maybe the service with high calls is the one with the problem. For example, \'frontend-proxy\' being called 15 times by \'load-generator\' might be under stress. Checking the metrics for \'frontend-proxy\' could show high latency or errors. \n\nBut the user\'s previous get_metrics attempts had syntax errors. Maybe simplifying the query again. For example, checking the number of errors for each service. But 