</tool_call> - base_agent.py:llm_thinking_step:68 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content="Monitor and diagnose an application consisting of **MANY** microservices. Some or none of the microservices have faults. Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster.  Carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, and identify what is the root cause of the fault.\nStop diagnosis once you've found the root cause of the faults.\nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAfter you finished, submit the detected faulty components to the orchestrator for evaluation. The submission should be a natural language description of the root cause of the failure.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e63a49f2-07a6-42de-aac2-ee017865d2f8'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\nOpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\nAn online shopping platform built with a microservices architecture, showcasing OpenTelemetry instrumentation for distributed tracing across services.\nIt belongs to this namespace:\nastronomy-shop\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind When choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name. Then, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation. You can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks. If you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately. If you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission. You will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7a47597d-4dee-4c30-9b23-69fdacdb5417'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to execute read-only kubectl commands.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The read-only kubectl command you want to execute in a CLI to manage a k8s cluster. It should start with "kubectl".\n        Available Read-only Commands:\n        "kubectl api-resources",\n        "kubectl api-version",\n        "kubectl auth can-i",\n        "kubectl cluster-info",\n        "kubectl describe",\n        "kubectl diff",\n        "kubectl events",\n        "kubectl explain",\n        "kubectl get",\n        "kubectl logs",\n        "kubectl options",\n        "kubectl top",\n        "kubectl version",\n        "kubectl config view",\n        "kubectl config current-context",\n        "kubectl config get",\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit"\n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n\n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark. Use natural language to describe the root cause of the failure.\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='68e10855-de86-4742-bcfe-542fef8927d9'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's start by understanding the problem. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application called OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is a microservices-based platform. The goal is to identify any faults and determine their root causes. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out what services are running in the cluster. The application is in the namespace 'astronomy-shop'. The tools available include 'get_services' which retrieves service names from Grafana. That seems like a good starting point because knowing the services will help me check each one for issues.\n\nSo, I'll use 'get_services' to list all the services in the Grafana instance. Once I have the list, I can then check each service's metrics, traces, and dependencies to see if there are any anomalies. For example, if a service is down or has high error rates, that could indicate a problem. \n\nAfter getting the services, maybe I'll use 'get_metrics' to look at real-time metrics like error rates or latency. If there are spikes in errors or high latency, that could point to faulty services. Then, using 'get_traces' for specific services to see if there are any trace data indicating issues like failed requests or long durations. \n\nAlso, the 'get_dependency_graph' could show dependencies between services, helping to identify if a problem in one service is affecting others. If a service isn't showing up in the dependency graph, that might mean it's not running or has issues. \n\nBut first, I need the list of services. So the first step is to call 'get_services' to get all the service names in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. That way, I can systematically check each service for potential faults.\n</think>\n\nget_services  \nTo begin diagnosing the application, I need to identify all the services running in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. The `get_services` tool will retrieve the list of service names from Grafana, providing a foundation for further analysis of their health, metrics, and dependencies. This is the first step in understanding the application's architecture and identifying potential fault points.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 432, 'prompt_tokens': 1492, 'total_tokens': 1924, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe32-3447-7761-bc2a-3dd8b73b7119-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1492, 'output_tokens': 432, 'total_tokens': 1924}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7e826682-cd9a-43dc-91ca-351028d0fff7'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application with many microservices. The application is called OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, and it\'s in the namespace astronomy-shop. The goal is to figure out if there are any faults and identify the root cause.\n\nIn the previous step, I chose the get_services tool to retrieve the list of service names from Grafana. Now, I need to generate a tool call for that. The get_services tool doesn\'t require any arguments, so the command should be straightforward. The response will give me the list of services, which is essential to know which services are part of the application. Once I have the service names, I can proceed to check each one\'s health, metrics, and dependencies. \n\nWait, but the user mentioned that some or none of the microservices might have faults. So, after getting the services, the next step would be to check their metrics, maybe look into logs, or check their dependencies. But first, I need the list of services. The get_services tool is the right choice here. Let me make sure I\'m using the correct tool and parameters. The tool doesn\'t need any parameters, so the call should be just invoking get_services. \n\nI should also remember that the user is in the thinking stage, and the next step is to generate the tool call. So the correct JSON object would be {"name": "get_services", "arguments": {}}. That\'s all. No arguments needed. Once I get the services, I can move on to other tools like get_metrics or get_dependency_graph. But for now, the first step is to list all services in the namespace. \n\nWait, but the get_services tool\'s description says it retrieves service names from Grafana. Does that mean it\'s specific to the astronomy-shop namespace? The user mentioned the namespace, but the tool\'s description doesn\'t specify. Maybe I should check if there\'s a way to filter by namespace, but according to the tool\'s parameters, there\'s no namespace argument. Hmm. Maybe the Grafana instance is already scoped to the namespace, or perhaps the tool returns all services in the cluster. But the user specified the namespace, so maybe I need to use kubectl to get services in that namespace. Wait, but the available tools include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. \n\nWait, the user\'s initial instructions say to use the provided tools. The get_services tool is part of the available tools. But if the get_services tool doesn\'t take a namespace parameter, maybe it\'s not filtering correctly. However, the user mentioned the namespace is astronomy-shop, so perhaps the Grafana instance is already set to that namespace, or the tool\'s response includes services from that namespace. \n\nBut since the tool\'s parameters don\'t include a namespace, I have to assume that the get_services tool returns all services in the cluster. However, the user is focusing on the astronomy-shop namespace. Maybe I should first use kubectl to get the services in that namespace. But the available tools include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. So perhaps I should use that to run \'kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop\' to get the list. \n\nWait, but the user\'s initial step was to use get_services. Maybe the get_services tool is designed to get services from Grafana, which might be a different source. But the user\'s application is in a specific namespace. This is a bit confusing. However, the user\'s first step was to use get_services, so maybe I should proceed with that. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the get_services tool is part of the monitoring stack and returns services that are being monitored. But without more info, I have to follow the user\'s initial choice. So the correct tool call is get_services with no arguments. \n\nSo the tool call would be:\n\n{"name": "get_services", "arguments": {}}\n\nThat\'s the correct step here. Once I have the list of services, I can then check each one\'s metrics, dependencies, and logs. But for now, the first step is to get the services.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_services'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9fc83cf07ea07a62', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 852, 'prompt_tokens': 2473, 'total_tokens': 3325, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe32-491c-79f0-954d-40bb91cd5ea3-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_services', 'args': {}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9fc83cf07ea07a62', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2473, 'output_tokens': 852, 'total_tokens': 3325}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[\'cart\', \'flagd\', \'product-catalog\', \'image-provider\', \'jaeger-all-in-one\', \'ad\', \'payment\', \'frontend-proxy\', \'checkout\', \'frontend\', \'shipping\', \'load-generator\', \'accounting\', \'currency\', \'email\', \'fraud-detection\', \'quote\']", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='f430af95-6836-4d5c-b314-1cca47b72c1f', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9fc83cf07ea07a62'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c89525d0-4112-48a8-9f80-90d669d8ff70'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, so the user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is a microservices application. They\'ve already used the get_services tool to list all the services in the \'astronomy-shop\' namespace. The response included a list of services like \'cart\', \'flagd\', \'product-catalog\', etc. Now, the next step is to figure out which of these services might be faulty.\n\nSince the application has many microservices, the first thing is to check the health of each service. The user needs to determine if any services are not functioning correctly. The get_metrics tool can be used here. By querying metrics from Prometheus, they can check for errors, latency, or other anomalies. For example, looking at error rates or request latencies for each service could indicate a problem.\n\nAlternatively, using get_traces might help identify issues in the distributed tracing. If a service is causing delays or errors, traces can show where the bottlenecks are. But first, it\'s better to check the metrics to get an overview. Another option is to check the deployments and pods using kubectl commands to see if there are any deployment issues, like failed pods or scaling problems. However, the user might not have access to kubectl, but the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool is available. \n\nWait, the user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool. They could use it to get deployments and pods. For example, running \'kubectl get deployments\' in the \'astronomy-shop\' namespace would show the status of each deployment. If a deployment has zero replicas or is in a failed state, that\'s a red flag. Similarly, checking pods with \'kubectl get pods\' would show if any pods are crashing or not running. \n\nBut the user might not know which specific services are having issues. So, maybe start by checking the metrics for each service. Let\'s say they use get_metrics with a query like \'sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="astronomy-shop"}[5m]))\' to see the number of requests. If a service has a sudden drop in requests, that could indicate a problem. Alternatively, checking for errors with \'sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="astronomy-shop", status="5xx"}[5m]))\' to see if there are error rates. \n\nAlternatively, using get_dependency_graph could show if there are any broken dependencies between services. If a service isn\'t communicating properly with others, the dependency graph might show that. But the dependency graph might not be as immediate as checking metrics. \n\nThe user\'s goal is to find the root cause of any faults. So, the first step after getting the list of services is to check their health via metrics. The get_metrics tool allows querying Prometheus, which is commonly used for monitoring. So, the next logical step is to use get_metrics to check for any anomalies in the services. \n\nBut the user needs to choose a specific query. Maybe start with a general query to check for errors or latency. For example, using \'sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="astronomy-shop"}[5m]))\' to see the request count, or \'max(http_request_duration_seconds{job="astronomy-shop"})\' to check for high latency. However, the exact metrics depend on how the services are instrumented. Since the application uses OpenTelemetry, the metrics might be under a specific job name. \n\nAlternatively, using get_traces for a specific service to see if there are any traces that are taking too long or failing. But without knowing which service is faulty, it\'s better to first check metrics. \n\nSo, the best next step is to use get_metrics with a query that can detect errors or high latency across all services. The user can then analyze the results to identify which services are problematic. If the metrics show an issue, they can then drill down into specific services using get_traces or other tools. \n\nTherefore, the tool to use now is get_metrics with a query that checks for errors or latency. The query could be something like \'sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="astronomy-shop", status="5xx"}[5m]))\' to check for 5xx errors. Alternatively, checking for high latency with \'max(http_request_duration_seconds{job="astronomy-shop"})\'. \n\nBut the user might not know the exact metric names. However, since the application uses OpenTelemetry, the metrics might be under a specific namespace or job. Assuming the job is \'astronomy-shop\', the query can be adjusted accordingly. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be get_metrics with a query that checks for errors or latency. That would help identify which services are having issues. Once the metrics are analyzed, the user can focus on the problematic services and use get_traces or other tools to find the root cause.\n</think>\n\nget_metrics  \nTo identify potential faults, I need to check real-time metrics for anomalies such as high error rates, latency spikes, or resource exhaustion. Using `get_metrics` with a query targeting error counts or latency will help pinpoint problematic services. For example, querying 5xx error rates or request durations can reveal failing services or bottlenecks. This step is critical to narrow down which services require deeper investigation.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1076, 'prompt_tokens': 1726, 'total_tokens': 2802, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe32-71a5-7750-b820-0b1c01fd41df-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1726, 'output_tokens': 1076, 'total_tokens': 2802}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='54e9db7b-6988-49fe-a7db-115e4b5877a6'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose faults in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which has many microservices. They started by getting the list of services using get_services, which returned a list including cart, flagd, product-catalog, and others. Now, they want to check metrics to find any anomalies.\n\nThe next step was to use get_metrics. The assistant needs to decide on a PromQL query. Since the user mentioned checking for errors and latency, a good approach is to look for 5xx error rates, which indicate server-side errors. Also, checking request durations for high latency could show performance issues. \n\nSo, the query should include both metrics. The namespace is astronomy-shop, so the PromQL should filter by that. The assistant should use a query that captures these aspects. For example, sum by (service) (rate(http_server_errors_total{status=~"5..", namespace="astronomy-shop"}[5m])) for error rates and max by (service) (http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{namespace="astronomy-shop"}[5m]) for latency. \n\nBut the user\'s previous step was to get metrics, and the tool requires a query. The assistant needs to construct a query that covers both error rates and latency. Let me make sure the query is correct. Also, the time range should be recent, like 5 minutes, to get the latest data. \n\nSo the tool call would be get_metrics with the query combining both error and latency metrics. That way, the user can see if any services have high errors or slow responses, which are common fault indicators.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "sum by (service) (rate(http_server_errors_total{status=~\\"5..\\", namespace=\\"astronomy-shop\\"} [5m])) or max by (service) (http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{namespace=\\"astronomy-shop\\"} [5m])"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8698f7f7b4c097e7', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 408, 'prompt_tokens': 2714, 'total_tokens': 3122, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe32-a280-71c2-99df-9dc8affde306-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'sum by (service) (rate(http_server_errors_total{status=~"5..", namespace="astronomy-shop"} [5m])) or max by (service) (http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{namespace="astronomy-shop"} [5m])'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8698f7f7b4c097e7', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2714, 'output_tokens': 408, 'total_tokens': 3122}), ToolMessage(content="('[prom_mcp] Error querying get_metrics: 400 Client Error: Bad Request for url: http://prometheus-server.observe.svc.cluster.local:80/api/v1/query?query=sum+by+%28service%29+%28rate%28http_server_errors_total%7Bstatus%3D~%225..%22%2C+namespace%3D%22astronomy-shop%22%7D+%5B5m%5D%29%29+or+max+by+%28service%29+%28http_request_duration_seconds_bucket%7Bnamespace%3D%22astronomy-shop%22%7D+%5B5m%5D%29', 149)", id='6d3bf376-5946-43cd-bcf0-ac31c505dd0c', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8698f7f7b4c097e7'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5eafb58a-56e9-4370-8d70-7a7009f25207'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, the user is trying to monitor and diagnose the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application, which consists of many microservices. They\'ve already tried using get_services to list all services and get_metrics to check for errors and latency, but the get_metrics call resulted in a 400 Bad Request error. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out why the get_metrics query failed. The error message indicates a problem with the PromQL query. The original query was combining two parts with "or": sum by service of http_server_errors and max by service of http_request_duration. Maybe the syntax is incorrect. In PromQL, "or" isn\'t a valid operator for combining queries. Instead, you should use parentheses to group them or use a different approach.\n\nSo, the correct approach would be to split the query into two separate parts and check each one individually. That way, if one part fails, the other can still provide useful information. Let me adjust the query to check for 5xx errors first. The correct PromQL for summing 5xx errors by service would be sum by (service) (rate(http_server_errors_total{status~"5..", namespace="astronomy-shop"}[5m])).\n\nNext, I should check if the metrics names and labels are correct. The user mentioned the namespace is "astronomy-shop", so ensuring that the query includes the correct namespace is important. Also, verifying that the metrics http_server_errors_total and http_request_duration_seconds_bucket exist in the Prometheus instance would help. If those metrics aren\'t available, the query will fail.\n\nSince the initial query had a syntax error, splitting it into two separate queries would help isolate the issue. Let me first check the 5xx errors. If that works, then proceed to check the request duration. This step-by-step approach can help identify which part of the query is causing the problem and gather necessary metrics without overwhelming the system.\n\nAnother possibility is that the Prometheus server isn\'t properly exposing the required metrics. But since the user is using the OpenTelemetry demo, it\'s likely that the metrics are available. However, the query syntax is crucial here. By breaking down the query and testing each part, the user can pinpoint the exact issue and gather the necessary data for further diagnosis.\n</think>\n\nget_metrics  \nThe previous query failed due to incorrect PromQL syntax. I\'ll simplify the query to first check for 5xx error rates (a common indicator of service failures) and then check request latency. This will help isolate potential fault points without complex aggregations.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "get_metrics", "arguments": {"query": "sum by (service) (rate(http_server_errors_total{status~\\"5..\\", namespace=\\"astronomy-shop\\"} [5m]))"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 569, 'prompt_tokens': 2109, 'total_tokens': 2678, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe32-baa0-7692-8936-65ffa8b093bb-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2109, 'output_tokens': 569, 'total_tokens': 2678}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='27e2135d-91b3-4eaf-bbe2-bb4eaa9f5853'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose faults in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application, which has many microservices. They\'ve already tried using the get_metrics tool with a PromQL query to check for 5xx errors and request durations, but the initial query had a syntax error. Now, after simplifying the query to just check 5xx errors, the tool call was made.\n\nThe response from the tool was an error again, but the user\'s last action was to generate a tool call for get_metrics with the simplified query. Wait, actually, looking back, the user\'s last tool call was to get_metrics with the sum by (service) of rate(http_server_errors_total...). The response from the tool was a 400 error, which suggests that the PromQL query might still have issues. Maybe the syntax is incorrect, or the metrics names aren\'t matching.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that the query is too complex. Let me check the PromQL syntax. The user\'s query is "sum by (service) (rate(http_server_errors_total{status~\\"5..\\", namespace=\\"astronomy-shop\\"} [5m]))". The error might be due to the use of ~ for matching the status. In PromQL, the =~ operator is used for matching labels, but maybe the metric name is different. For example, maybe the correct metric is http_server_errors_total with status code labels, but the regex might not be correct. Alternatively, the namespace might not be correctly applied.\n\nAnother possibility is that the query is missing some parts. For example, the rate function requires a time range, which the user included as [5m]. But maybe the metric name is different. Let me think: in Prometheus, the metric for HTTP server errors might be something like http_server_requests_total or similar, with status codes as labels. If the actual metric is named differently, the query would fail.\n\nAlternatively, the user might need to check if the metrics are available. If the query is correct but the metrics aren\'t present, the result would be zero or no data. However, the error is a 400 Bad Request, which indicates a syntax error rather than a missing metric.\n\nSo, the next step is to adjust the query. Maybe the user should first check if the metric exists. For example, query for http_server_errors_total{namespace="astronomy-shop"} to see if it\'s present. If not, maybe the correct metric name is different. Alternatively, use a simpler query to check for any errors, like sum by (service) (count(http_server_errors_total{namespace="astronomy-shop"})) to see if there are any errors.\n\nAlternatively, the user could try a different approach. Since the initial get_services call gave a list of services, maybe they can check each service\'s metrics individually. For example, if a service is named \'cart\', query for its specific errors.\n\nBut given the previous error, the user might need to correct the PromQL syntax. Let me check the syntax again. The query is sum by (service) (rate(http_server_errors_total{status~\\"5..\\", namespace=\\"astronomy-shop\\"} [5m]))). The rate function is used correctly here. The status label is being matched with a regex that starts with 5, which is correct for 5xx errors. The namespace is correctly specified. Maybe the issue is that the metric name is different. For example, maybe it\'s http_server_requests_total with status code as a label. If that\'s the case, the correct metric would be http_server_requests_total, and the query would need to filter by status code.\n\nAlternatively, the user could try using a different metric, like http_requests_total or similar. If the metrics are not available, the query would return nothing, but the error would be a 400 if the syntax is wrong.\n\nAnother possibility is that the user is using the wrong namespace. The namespace in the query is "astronomy-shop", which matches the application\'s namespace. So that\'s correct.\n\nGiven that the user is getting a 400 error, the most likely issue is a syntax error in the PromQL query. Let me re-express the query. The correct syntax for a rate function is rate(metric_name{label="value"} [5m]). The user\'s query is sum by (service) (rate(http_server_errors_total{status~\\"5..\\", namespace=\\"astronomy-shop\\"} [5m]))). The syntax seems correct. Maybe the problem is that the status label is not correctly named. For example, if the label is called \'http_status\' instead of \'status\', the query would fail. But without knowing the exact metric structure, it\'s hard to say.\n\nAlternatively, the user could try a simpler query first, like checking the number of errors without the rate function. For example, sum by (service) (count(http_server_errors_total{namespace="astronomy-shop"})) to see if there are any errors. If that returns data, then the rate function can be applied. If not, the metric name might be incorrect.\n\nBut since the user is in the process of diagnosing, and the previous attempt failed, the next step is to adjust the query. Maybe the user should first check if the metric exists. So, the next tool call would be to get_metrics with a query that just checks for the existence of the metric, like http_server_errors_total{namespace="astronomy-shop"}.\n\nAlternatively, the user could try using a different metric name. For example, if the correct metric is http_server_requests_total, then the query would be sum by (service) (rate(http_server_requests_total{status~\\"5..\\", namespace=\\"astronomy-shop\\"} [5m]))).\n\nBut without knowing the exact metric names, it\'s challenging. The user might need to first check the available metrics in the Prometheus instance. However, the tools available don\'t include a way to list all metrics, so the user has to guess based on common names.\n\nGiven the previous error, the user might need to simplify the query further. Let\'s try removing the status filter and see if the query returns any data. For example, sum by (service) (rate(http_server_errors_total{namespace="astronomy-shop"} [5m]))). If that works, then the status filter is the issue. If not, the metric name is wrong.\n\nAlternatively, the user could check for any errors using a different approach, like checking the number of errors per service. But given the time constraints, the best next step is to adjust the query to check for the existence of the metric first, then proceed with the rate function.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "sum by (service) (count(http_server_errors_total{namespace=\\"astronomy-shop\\"} ))"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-81d4c8a6df61610b', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1414, 'prompt_tokens': 3116, 'total_tokens': 4530, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_t