r angle: the user checked the pods, but maybe the deployments are not correctly configured. For example, if a deployment has a wrong image or a misconfigured replica count. But the kubectl get pods shows all are running. \n\nWait, the user might need to check the logs of the services to see if there are any errors. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can be used to get logs. For example, checking the logs of the \'cart\' service to see if there are any exceptions or errors. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the services are not properly exposed. Using the get_services tool again to check if all services are listed in Grafana. But the initial get_services already listed all the services. \n\nWait, the user might need to check the service endpoints. For example, using kubectl get services in the namespace to see if the services are correctly exposed. But the user hasn\'t done that yet. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the issue is with the OpenTelemetry collector or Jaeger. The jaeger-all-in-one service is running, but maybe it\'s not collecting traces properly. Checking the logs of the Jaeger pod could help. \n\nBut the user\'s last step was checking the pods, and all are running. So maybe the next step is to check the logs of a specific service, like \'cart\', to see if there are any errors. Using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with kubectl logs to check the logs. \n\nSo the next tool should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command to get logs of a critical service, like \'cart\', to look for any runtime errors or issues.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo investigate potential runtime errors or logs indicating issues, I\'ll check the logs of the \'cart\' service, which is a core component. This will reveal any exceptions, errors, or warnings that might not be captured in metrics or traces.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl logs cart-65474cd577-7bpt5 -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider