     serviceAccountName: astronomy-shop\n      terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 30\n      volumes:\n      - emptyDir: {}\n        name: config-rw\n      - configMap:\n          defaultMode: 420\n          name: flagd-config\n        name: config-ro\nstatus:\n  availableReplicas: 1\n  conditions:\n  - lastTransitionTime: "2026-03-17T23:45:41Z"\n    lastUpdateTime: "2026-03-17T23:45:41Z"\n    message: Deployment has minimum availability.\n    reason: MinimumReplicasAvailable\n    status: "True"\n    type: Available\n  - lastTransitionTime: "2026-03-17T23:44:09Z"\n    lastUpdateTime: "2026-03-17T23:45:41Z"\n    message: ReplicaSet "flagd-79c4c6cb44" has successfully progressed.\n    reason: NewReplicaSetAvailable\n    status: "True"\n    type: Progressing\n  observedGeneration: 1\n  readyReplicas: 1\n  replicas: 1\n  updatedReplicas: 1\n', id='8b9d4ee6-ed3d-4534-be9a-dc63ea01bd0c', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9b5763f3b3846b3e'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='adcddf21-2515-4150-9c70-9163daf2cf93'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to fix the issue with the flagd service in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. They\'ve gone through several steps, including applying a deployment with readiness and liveness probes, but there were some issues with the namespace and the deployment configuration.\n\nFirst, the initial problem was that the flagd service wasn\'t detecting readiness failures because there were no probes configured. The user tried adding probes but faced issues with the namespace. The deployment was initially in the "sregym" namespace, but the application belongs to "astronomy-shop". So, the first step was to correct the namespace in the deployment.\n\nThen, there was an error when applying the updated deployment because the required annotation was missing. The user added the annotation and reapplied the deployment. Now, the deployment is in the correct namespace, and the YAML shows the probes are configured. However, the status shows "Progressing" and "MinimumReplicasAvailable", which might indicate that the deployment is still in a transitional state.\n\nThe next step is to check if the probes are actually working. The user tried to get the pod logs but encountered a "Not Found" error because the pod name was incorrect or the pod wasn\'t ready yet. Since the deployment is now in the correct namespace, the next logical step is to check the pod\'s status and logs again.\n\nBut the user can\'t use command substitution, so they need to first get the pod name. However, the previous attempt to get the pod name resulted in an error. Maybe the pod hasn\'t been created yet, or the deployment is still progressing. The status shows "availableReplicas: 1" and "readyReplicas: 1", which suggests the pod is available. \n\nAlternatively, the user should check the pod\'s readiness and liveness probe status. If the probes are correctly configured, the pod should be marked as ready. If there\'s still an issue, the logs would show why the probes are failing. But since the user can\'t get the logs due to the pod name issue, they might need to wait for the deployment to stabilize or check the pod\'s status again.\n\nAnother possibility is that the `/health` endpoint isn\'t accessible, causing the probes to fail. The user should verify that the service is correctly exposing the endpoint. However, without access to the service\'s configuration or logs, it\'s hard to tell. \n\nGiven the current state, the best approach is to check the pod\'s status again to ensure it\'s running and ready. If the pod is running, then the logs can be retrieved. If not, the deployment might need further adjustments. The user should also verify that the service is correctly configured to route traffic to the pod and that the probes are correctly set up.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \n**Reasoning:** The deployment is now in the correct namespace (`astronomy-shop`) and has the required probes. The status shows `availableReplicas: 1` and `readyReplicas: 1`, indicating the pod is running and ready. However, the earlier log retrieval failed due to an invalid pod name. To confirm the fix, retrieve the pod name again and verify the logs to ensure the probes are functioning correctly.  \n\n**Next step:** Get the pod name in the correct namespace and check its logs to validate probe success.  \n\ntool_call:  \n```json\n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "command": "kubectl get pod -l opentelemetry.io/name=flagd -n astronomy-shop -o jsonpath=\'{.items[0].metadata.name}\'"\n}\n```', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 746, 'prompt_tokens': 9899, 'total_tokens': 10645, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe37-4fe5-7fe1-9832-751ad65174c2-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 9899, 'output_tokens': 746, 'total_tokens': 10645}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='63132bf1-da7c-450e-92fc-f708bd6fb369'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to fix an issue with the flagd deployment in their Kubernetes cluster. They\'ve gone through several steps to apply the necessary changes, including adding readiness and liveness probes, correcting the namespace, and handling the kubectl apply errors. Now, they\'re trying to get the pod name to check the logs.\n\nIn the previous steps, they tried using `kubectl get pod -l app=flagd` but got an error because the pod wasn\'t found. Then they checked the deployment\'s namespace and realized it was set to "sregym" instead of "astronomy-shop". They corrected that and reapplied the deployment. Now, the deployment is in the correct namespace, and the status shows the pod is available.\n\nThe next step is to get the pod name in the correct namespace. The user used `kubectl get pod -l opentelemetry.io/name=flagd -n astronomy-shop -o jsonpath=\'{.items[0].metadata.name}\'` to retrieve the pod name. This command uses the label selector `opentelemetry.io/name=flagd` which matches the pod\'s label from the deployment\'s template. The `-n astronomy-shop` ensures it\'s in the correct namespace. The `jsonpath` extracts the first pod\'s name.\n\nOnce they have the pod name, they can then use `kubectl logs` to check the logs and verify if the probes are working as expected. If the logs show that the probes are succeeding, then the issue is resolved. If not, they might need to adjust the probe configurations or check the application\'s health endpoints.\n\nSo, the next tool call should be to get the pod name using the correct label and namespace. After that, they can check the logs to confirm everything is working properly.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pod -l opentelemetry.io/name=flagd -n astronomy-shop -o jsonpath=\'{.items[0].metadata.name}\'"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8f5a84cf82f2a849', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 428, 'prompt_tokens': 11104, 'total_tokens': 11532, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe37-7536-7e51-986e-6eae8af50fdf-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': "kubectl get pod -l opentelemetry.io/name=flagd -n astronomy-shop -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}'"}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8f5a84cf82f2a849', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11104, 'output_tokens': 428, 'total_tokens': 11532}), ToolMessage(content='flagd-79c4c6cb44-gmnww', id='1d9829ce-64ea-43ca-bf53-ee33188d69d8', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8f5a84cf82f2a849'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='3fe09a94-9bb9-4641-866c-a644c936da0b'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to fix the issue with the flagd service in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. They\'ve already applied the necessary changes to add readiness and liveness probes to the deployment. The deployment is now in the correct namespace, and the status shows that the replicas are available and ready. \n\nThe next step was to get the pod name using the label `opentelemetry.io/name=flagd` in the `astronomy-shop` namespace. The tool call returned the pod name `flagd-79c4c6cb44-gmnww`. Now, the user needs to check the logs of this pod to ensure that the probes are working correctly. \n\nThe previous attempt to get the logs failed because the pod name was incorrect or the namespace was wrong. But now that the pod name is correctly retrieved from the right namespace, the next logical step is to fetch the logs. This will help verify if the probes are succeeding or if there are any errors preventing them from doing so. \n\nThe user should use the `exec_kubectl_cmd_safely` tool again to run the `kubectl logs` command with the correct pod name and namespace. This will allow them to see the actual logs and confirm that the probes are functioning as expected. If the logs show that the probes are passing, then the issue is resolved. If not, they can further investigate based on the log output.\n\nSo, the correct tool call is to get the logs of the specified pod in the correct namespace. This will provide the necessary information to confirm whether the root cause has been addressed.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \n**Reasoning:** The pod name `flagd-79c4c6cb44-gmnww` is now valid in the `astronomy-shop` namespace. To confirm the probes are functioning, retrieve the pod logs to verify if the `/health` endpoint is responding correctly and if the readiness/liveness checks are succeeding.  \n\n**Next step:** Fetch the logs of the `flagd` pod to validate probe behavior.  \n\ntool_call:  \n```json\n{\n  "tool": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely",\n  "command": "kubectl logs flagd-79c4c6cb44-gmnww -n astronomy-shop"\n}\n```', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 483, 'prompt_tokens': 10208, 'total_tokens': 10691, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe37-8dfc-7332-9b07-9ff2fce340d9-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10208, 'output_tokens': 483, 'total_tokens': 10691}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e27df0c5-a97a-47ee-bc8b-ce12008834c0'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me recap what's been done so far. The user was trying to fix an issue with the `flagd` service in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. The initial problem was that the service wasn't detecting readiness failures because there were no probes configured. The user applied a deployment with readiness and liveness probes, but there were some issues with the namespace and the pod name.\n\nFirst, they tried applying the deployment but got errors because the namespace was wrong. Then, they corrected the namespace to `astronomy-shop` and reapplied the deployment. The deployment status showed the pod was available, but when trying to get logs, the pod name was incorrect. After getting the correct pod name, the next step was to check the logs to ensure the probes are working.\n\nNow, the user is about to call the `kubectl logs` command with the correct pod name and namespace. The previous attempts to get logs failed because the pod name was wrong, but now the pod name is valid. The logs should show if the `/health` endpoint is responding as expected, which would indicate that the probes are functioning correctly. If the logs show the health checks passing, then the issue is resolved. If not, further troubleshooting might be needed, like checking the endpoint's availability or probe configurations. But based on the current steps, the user is verifying the final part of the fix by checking the logs.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs flagd-79c4c6cb44-gmnww -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-af641a37252fbf48', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 333, 'prompt_tokens': 11383, 'total_tokens': 11716, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe37-a5f1-7f83-8a99-24175f12b666-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs flagd-79c4c6cb44-gmnww -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-af641a37252fbf48', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11383, 'output_tokens': 333, 'total_tokens': 11716}), ToolMessage(content='\n\t\t ______   __       ________   _______    ______      \n\t\t/_____/\\ /_/\\     /_______/\\ /______/\\  /_____/\\     \n\t\t\\::::_\\/_\\:\\ \\    \\::: _  \\ \\\\::::__\\/__\\:::_ \\ \\    \n\t\t \\:\\/___/\\\\:\\ \\    \\::(_)  \\ \\\\:\\ /____/\\\\:\\ \\ \\ \\   \n\t\t  \\:::._\\/ \\:\\ \\____\\:: __  \\ \\\\:\\\\_  _\\/ \\:\\ \\ \\ \\  \n\t\t   \\:\\ \\    \\:\\/___/\\\\:.\\ \\  \\ \\\\:\\_\\ \\ \\  \\:\\/.:| | \n\t\t    \\_\\/     \\_____\\/ \\__\\/\\__\\/ \\_____\\/   \\____/_/ \n\n2026-03-17T23:45:36.972Z\tinfo\tcmd/start.go:143\tflagd version: v0.12.9 (1ee636aa039ad862f055831bd2ab42216fe30865), built at: 2025-07-28\t{"component": "start"}\n2026-03-17T23:45:36.973Z\tinfo\tflag-sync/sync_service.go:98\tstarting flag sync service on port 8015\t{"component": "FlagSyncService"}\n2026-03-17T23:45:36.974Z\tinfo\tfile/filepath_sync.go:60\tStarting filepath sync notifier\t{"component": "sync", "sync": "fsnotify"}\n2026-03-17T23:45:36.974Z\tinfo\tflag-evaluation/connect_service.go:251\tmetrics and probes listening at 8014\t{"component": "service"}\n2026-03-17T23:45:36.974Z\tinfo\tofrep/ofrep_service.go:58\tofrep service listening at 8016\t{"component": "OFREPService"}\n2026-03-17T23:45:36.975Z\tinfo\tfile/filepath_sync.go:99\twatching filepath: ./etc/flagd/demo.flagd.json\t{"component": "sync", "sync": "fsnotify"}\n2026-03-17T23:45:36.976Z\tinfo\tflag-evaluation/connect_service.go:231\tFlag IResolver listening at [::]:8013\t{"component": "service"}\n', id='e50e54d3-1a5b-4f9b-8f3e-6f85c749b9d5', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-af641a37252fbf48'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d3438277-5937-4811-9f8a-7492cbdec0c9')]]
